AB  - Fine particulate matters (PM2.5) emitted from gasoline vehicles have gained widespread attention due to their adverse impacts on human health and atmospheric environment. Fuel properties are critical to the formation of PM2.5 from gasoline engines and understanding of their effects is the prerequisite for the formulation of stricter fuel standards. In this study, the influence of aromatic content, olefin content and T90 (90 %v distillation temperature) on the particulate matter (PM) mass, particle number (PN), carbonaceous components and particle- bound polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) emissions from both port fuel injection (PFI) and gasoline direct injection (GDI) vehicles were investigated across dynamometer test. Results showed aromatic content has greater impacts on particulate emissions compared to olefin content and T90, with 40 % aromatic content fuel resulting in 11.4-57.0 % higher PM mass, 63.1-72.5 % more PN emissions and 25.4-80.9 % more carbonaceous components emissions compared with 20% aromatic content fuel. Higher aromatic content also caused the particle size distribution (PSD) of GDI vehicles to shift towards larger accumulation mode (AM) particles. T90 exhibited similar effects with aromatic content, but having little impacts on the PSD. The influence of olefins on particle emissions was modest and more complex. For PFI vehicles, high olefin content was associated with 19.9 % lower PM mass emissions and 16.9-19.6 % lower carbonaceous components, while the opposite results were observed for GDI vehicles. Moreover, olefin content exhibited little effect on PN emission for both PFI vehicles and GDI vehicles. Analysis using a Random Forest model indicated that PAH emissions from particulate matter were primarily influenced by the content of heavy aromatics, rather than the total aromatic content, and thereby in their toxicity. The findings in this study will provide insights and guidelines for formulating stricter fuel standards, thereby reducing tailpipe particle emissions.
