AB  - Intensive poultry breeding and egg production in China is rapidly growing, yet the monitoring and management for the air pollution was lagged, particularly for fine particulate matter (PM2.5)-bound metals, which poses health risks to both breeders and poultry, with existing detection methods being time-consuming. In this study, PM2.5 inside and outside a typical tunnel-ventilated layer house were collected, and laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy (LIBS) were used for rapidly detection and risk assessment of PM2.5-bound metals, including aluminum (Al), titanium (Ti), chromium (Cr), manganese (Mn), iron (Fe), copper (Cu), zinc (Zn), arsenic (As), strontium (Sr), cadmium (Cd), barium (Ba) and lead (Pb). The carbon fingerprint peak ratio (CFPR) correction was proposed for reducing the matrix effect of PM2.5 generated from different sources. Machine learning models were built for quantitative analysis of PM2.5 concentration and the relative content (w/w) of metals in PM2.5. The optimal CFPR-LIBS-based models performed well with R(2)p(sample) over 0.94. The PM2.5-bound metal concentrations in air (w/v) were then determined, and the NRMSE for 12 elements were 0.0402 similar to 0.1397. The environmental and health risks associated with PM2.5-bound metals at the layer house were assessed and visualized utilizing LIBS, and the high-risk areas were identified. The results indicated that though heavy metals in feed and manure were under safety threshold, Cu, Pb, Cd, Mn, Cr and As were found to be bound by PM2.5 with high enrichment degree and have potential health or environmental risks, and the feed formulations and management policy should be improved. The proposed rapid analysis, risk assessment and mapping method using LIBS could greatly improve the timeliness of PM2.5 detection and air quality assessment in poultry houses, providing valuable reference for breeders to achieve efficient and green egg production.
