AB  - The air quality of indoor living and working environments may be affected by known or unknown sources. Understanding the spatiotemporal distribution characteristics of indoor air quality (IAQ) helps in tracing the sources, understanding diffusion patterns, and provides a basis for pollution control, individual protection, and exposure assessment. By integrating advanced high-precision indoor positioning technology, this study develops an indoor monitoring method and has designed a low-cost PM2.5 sensor (LCS) for this purpose. To assess the feasibility of this method and evaluate its strengths and weaknesses, an indoor case study targeting PM2.5 as the primary pollutant was conducted. The method effectively captured the variations in PM2.5 concentrations with very high spatiotemporal resolution, originating from kitchen cooking, outdoor sources, and the living room. Additionally, interpretable machine learning (ML) was employed to analyze the contributions of various factors-such as pollution sources, window status, temperature, and humidity to PM2.5 concentrations in the living room. The results demonstrate that this approach can achieve high-resolution spatiotemporal distribution of IAQ and subsequently pinpoint the location and intensity of indoor sources. The method also exhibits strong resistance to environmental interference, and is easy to deploy in arbitrary indoor spaces for long-term indoor operation.
