TI  - A spatiotemporal deep learning ensemble for multi-step PM2.5 prediction: A case study of Bangkok metropolitan region in Thailand
T2  - ATMOSPHERIC POLLUTION RESEARCH
LA  - English
KW  - PM2.5 concentrations
KW  - Air quality forecasting
KW  - Spatiotemporal model
KW  - Ensemble deep learning
KW  - Satellite-based information
KW  - PM2.5
KW  - PM10
AB  - PM2.5 air pollution poses a global health and environmental crisis, with major impacts on human health, ecosystems, and climate change. Thailand, like many regions worldwide, experiences significant challenges from elevated PM2.5 levels, necessitating effective mitigation strategies. This study addressed this critical issue by developing a novel spatiotemporal deep learning ensemble model for multi-step PM(2.5 )prediction. By integrating recurrent and recurrent-free neural network architectures, our model effectively captured intricate spatiotemporal patterns in PM2.5 concentrations. Furthermore, the incorporation of satellite-derived Aerosol Optical Depth, meteorological data, and land-use information enhances the model's ability to represent external factors contributing to PM2.5 levels. The ensemble approach, integrating diverse neural network architectures, demonstrated superior performance in predicting PM2.5 concentrations at various time horizons compared to individual models. Particularly, the best ensemble models demonstrated mean absolute errors of 4.73, 5.71, and 6.54 mu g/m(3) for 6, 12, and 24 h ahead prediction, respectively. This research offers contributes to the field of air quality forecasting. By providing accurate and timely PM2.5 predictions, our model empowers decision-makers to implement effective mitigation strategies to improve public health, environmental quality, and overall wellbeing.
