AB  - Exposure to airborne pollutants and bioaerosols represents a major health risk, particularly through inhalation in occupational and public settings. In the absence of effective control measures, cloth masks serve as a practical barrier against aerosol penetration. This study investigates the filtration performance of cloth masks by modeling the influence of fabric parameters using an artificial neural network (ANN). A dataset of 70 masks, tested under standardized laboratory conditions, was used to train and validate the model. Seven input parameters (including fabric composition, mass per unit area, surface wetting, dyeing treatment, and air permeability) were analyzed in relation to their effect on the filtration efficiency of 3 mu m particles. Trend analysis revealed that water repellency, fabric structure, and material type significantly affect filtration performance. The ANN model showed strong predictive capability and outperformed a linear baseline. These findings offer practical insights for the textile industry by supporting data-driven design of protective cloth masks and highlight the potential of ANN in optimizing filtration performance through material engineering.
