AB  - The concentration of lung deposition surface area (LDSA) is a crucial metric for assessing potential health impacts associated with particle size distribution. Traditional LDSA sensors, which rely on particle charging, are limited to measuring particles up to 300 nm in size. However, aerosols in the submicrometer to micrometer range are also significant for evaluating exposure risks. In this study, we introduce an optical method for LDSA sensing that leverages light-scattering angular spectrum and deep learning techniques. Our approach utilizes a compact, lens-free sensor to sample angular spectrum signals, which are then processed by a ResNet-like neural network to adjust the conversion factor between LDSA concentration and scattering light intensity. Experimental results demonstrate that our prototype achieves a maximum relative error of 14.78% in measuring the angular spectrum. Additionally, the neural network compensates for variations in particle size distribution and refractive index, refining the conversion factor. When compared to reference LDSA concentrations provided by laboratory instruments, our prototype maintains relative errors within 17.66%. With its simple mechanical design, high accuracy, and cost-effectiveness, this method presents a promising solution for online and field measurements of LDSA concentration.
