AB  - Understanding the transport patterns of aerosols between urban and rural areas and predicting their spatiotemporal variations is crucial. In this study, from June 2022 to December 2023, ground-based Multi-Axis Differential Optical Absorption Spectroscopy (MAX-DOAS) observations were conducted in urban and rural areas of Hefei, Anhui, China. Anhui University (AHU) and Changfeng (CF) were selected as representative urban and rural sites, respectively. The correlation coefficient between the MAX-DOAS aerosol extinction measurements at the lower layer and in situ PM2.5 at AHU reached 0.720. The study found that the seasonal average aerosol profiles at AHU exhibited a Gaussian pattern, while those at CF showed both Gaussian and band-like distributions, with the maximum aerosol loading appearing within 1200 m. Seasonal average aerosol vertical profiles indicated that in spring, summer, and autumn, peak aerosol extinction values at AHU occurred approximately two hours earlier than at CF. In winter, however, peak aerosol extinction values at CF appeared about two hours earlier than at AHU. The study also revealed the phenomenon of high-altitude aerosol transport during the observation period. Typical transport cases were selected for each season: August 21, October 1, December 13, 2022, and April 9, 2023. It was found that the main aerosol transport height ranged from 300 to 1200 m, with aerosol transport fluxes ranging from 1.744 to 3.954 km- 1 center dot m center dot s- 1. Potential source analysis indicated that aerosols at AHU and CF primarily originated from nearby urban areas, with significant transport from southeastern Henan, eastern Hubei, northern Jiangxi, and northwestern Zhejiang, with relatively short transport distances. Additionally, using a deep learning-based Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model, we predicted the daily average aerosol vertical profiles at the CF from July to December 2023. The correlation between the predicted data and the actual MAX-DOAS observations at CF reached 0.750, demonstrating the model's accuracy in spatiotemporal prediction. Predicting daily average aerosol vertical profiles can provide early warnings of aerosol pollution events and support the formulation and implementation of environmental policies.
