AB  - Electric vehicles are increasingly acknowledged as a promising solution for addressing global warming and climate change, with their effectiveness greatly influenced by the attributes of energy storage systems. Although lithium-ion batteries are presently the primary choice for electric vehicle energy storage, their deterioration over time poses challenges in maintaining performance. This research introduces a technique for accurately forecasting battery health, which is crucial for ensuring safety and gaining consumer trust. By utilizing various machine learning algorithms such as Random Forest Regression, Light Gradient Boosting Regression, Support Vector Regression, and Stacked Regressor, this study investigates advancements in estimating the state of health of batteries. Feature extraction and selection, and Pearson correlation analysis, play a significant role, with seven key variables identified as inputs for prediction models, categorically capturing essential aspects of battery performance. The characteristics categorized within class A comprised the Voltage-time graph first turning point, Voltage-time graph second turning point, two turning point slop, and turning points in the voltage-time graph yielding superior outcomes compared to other categorized features. The study illustrates the effectiveness of the proposed method using the experimental battery dataset, achieving an impressive SOH prediction accuracy of 99.36 % with the Stacked regression model. This multi-feature model signifies a substantial progression in addressing existing limitations in lithium-ion batteries' SOH estimation, offering potential avenues for enhancing electric vehicle performance and sustainability.
